{"ex-3B06-1":{"number":"1","title":"Estimating the wavelength","question":"<p>A red laser and a blue laser are directed towards a double slit of slit separation 0.3 mm in turn. The figures below show the interference patterns produced on a screen 6 m from the double slit.</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/30f6808dc9c91c4a189df5cd.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:509.2px\"/></p><p>(a) State the advantage of measuring the separation between several fringes instead of two adjacent fringes in estimating the wavelength of the light.</p><p>(b) Estimate the fringe separation in each case.</p><p>(c) Estimate the wavelengths of the red light and the blue light.</p>","answer":"<p>(a) This reduces the percentage error in the measurement.</p><p>(b) For the red laser, fringe separation = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>4</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>4</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = 1.35 cm</p><p>For the blue laser, fringe separation = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>4</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = 0.8 cm</p><p>(c) By Δ<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>y</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>λ</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>D</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>a</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>, λ = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>a</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>Δ</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>y</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>D</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>.</p><p>Wavelength of red laser = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>(</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>×</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>–</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><mrow><mtext>)</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>(</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>×</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>–</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><mrow><mtext>)</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>6</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = 6.75 × 10<sup>−7</sup> m</p><p>Wavelength of blue laser = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>(</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>×</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>–</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><mrow><mtext>)</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>(</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>8</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>×</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>–</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><mrow><mtext>)</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>6</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = 4 × 10<sup>−7</sup> m</p>","tips":"<p><b>Tip</b></p><p>4(Δy) = 5.4 cm</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/5130bc2a049ed19133fa091f.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:178.9px\"/></p>","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 3B  |  Chapter 3B06  |  Source PDF p. 13","source":{"name":"3B06 Examples.docx","sha256":"d583ea1038f9b46820eba3be68c1c0973a5d80238ac0c6e625df0427ca7931ae","block":2},"id":"ex-3B06-1","chapter":"3B06"},"ex-3B06-2":{"number":"2","title":"Fringes formed by a grating","question":"<p>Light of wavelength 630 nm is directed towards a diffraction grating. The figure shows the pattern projected on a screen 1 m from the grating. The distance between the two second-order bright fringes is 72 cm.</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/4c43f1de5e806e131675f6fd.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:447.7px\"/></p><p>(a) Find the angle of diffraction θ<sub>2</sub> of the second-order bright fringe.</p><p>(b) Find the grating spacing <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>d</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>.</p><p>(c) Find the highest order of bright fringe.</p><p>(d) Find the total number of bright fringes formed.</p>","answer":"<p>(a) Distance of 2nd-order fringe from 0th-order fringe = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>7</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = 0.36 m</p><p>tan θ<sub>2</sub> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>6</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math></p><p>θ<sub>2</sub> = 19.8°</p><p>(b) By <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>d</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> sin θ<sub>n</sub> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>λ,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>d</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> sin 19.8° = (2)(630 × 10<sup>−9</sup>)</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>d</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 3.72 × 10<sup>−6</sup> m</p><p>(c) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> ≤ <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>d</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>λ</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>7</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>×</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>–</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>6</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>6</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>×</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>–</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>9</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = 5.9</p><p>∴ The highest order of bright fringe is 5.</p><p>(d) Total number of bright fringes = 5 × 2 + 1 = 11</p>","tips":"<p><b>Tip</b></p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/757adc761b6cd06e26692f9a.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:185.5px\"/></p>","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 3B  |  Chapter 3B06  |  Source PDF p. 21","source":{"name":"3B06 Examples.docx","sha256":"d583ea1038f9b46820eba3be68c1c0973a5d80238ac0c6e625df0427ca7931ae","block":20},"id":"ex-3B06-2","chapter":"3B06"},"ex-3B06-3":{"number":"3","title":"Multi-coloured interference pattern","question":"<p>Red light of wavelength 700 nm and violet light of wavelength 400 nm pass through a diffraction grating of 200 lines per mm.</p><p>(a) Find the angle of diffraction of the 2nd-order maxima (i.e. 2nd-order bright fringes) of the two lights.</p><p>(b) Find the angle of diffraction of the 3rd-order maxima</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/edb281763d47fe6b8a7eb472.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:307.5px\"/></p><p>of the two lights.</p><p>(c) Find the angle ϕ<sub>2</sub> between the 2nd-order maxima of the two lights.</p><p>(d) Find the angle ϕ<sub>3</sub> between the 3rd-order maxima of the two lights.</p>","answer":"<p>Grating spacing <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>d</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>×</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>–</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = 5 × 10<sup>−6</sup> m</p><p>Apply <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>d</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> sin θ<sub>n</sub> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>λ.</p><div class=\"study-table-wrap\"><table><tbody><tr><td><p>2nd-order maxima</p></td><td><p>3rd-order maxima</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>(a) For red light, (5 × 10<sup>−6</sup>) sin θ<sub>2R</sub> = (2) (700 × 10<sup>−9</sup>) θ<sub>2R</sub> = 16.3° For violet light, (5 × 10<sup>−6</sup>) sin θ<sub>2V</sub> = (2) (400 × 10<sup>−9</sup>) θ<sub>2V</sub> = 9.21°</p></td><td><p>(b) For red light, (5 × 10<sup>−6</sup>) sin θ<sub>3R</sub> = (3) (700 × 10<sup>−9</sup>) θ<sub>3R</sub> = 24.8° For violet light, (5 × 10<sup>−6</sup>) sin θ<sub>3V</sub> = (3) (400 × 10<sup>−9</sup>) θ<sub>3V</sub> = 13.9°</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>(c) ϕ<sub>2</sub> = 16.3° − 9.21° = 7.2°</p></td><td><p>(d) ϕ<sub>3</sub> = 24.8° − 13.9° = 10.9°</p></td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Exercise 6.3 Q5 (p.128)</p>","tips":"","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 3B  |  Chapter 3B06  |  Source PDF p. 22","source":{"name":"3B06 Examples.docx","sha256":"d583ea1038f9b46820eba3be68c1c0973a5d80238ac0c6e625df0427ca7931ae","block":41},"id":"ex-3B06-3","chapter":"3B06"},"ex-3B06-4":{"number":"4","title":"Estimating wavelength using a grating","question":"<p>Fanny looks at a red LED through a diffraction grating with grating spacing</p><p>4.35 × 10<sup>−6</sup> m and sees several bright fringes (Fig a). She asks her classmate</p><p>to put a pen at the position of a bright fringe and measures the distance <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>y</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> between the fringe and the LED. The angle θ<sub>n</sub> is then calculated. They repeat the measurement for several bright fringes and plot a graph of sin θ<sub>n</sub> against the order of fringe <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> (Fig b). What is the wavelength λ of the light?</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/f913b1c1a719169ec12346fe.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:489.4px\"/></p>","answer":"<p>By <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>d</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> sin θ<sub>n</sub> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>λ, sin θ<sub>n</sub> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>λ</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>d</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> × <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow></mrow></math></p><p>∴ Slope of graph = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>λ</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>d</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math></p><p>∴ λ = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>d</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> × slope of graph = 4.35 × 10<sup>−6</sup> × <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>6</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>–</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>4</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>–</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = 6.53 × 10<sup>−7</sup> m</p><p>Exercise 6.3 Q9 (p.129)</p>","tips":"","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 3B  |  Chapter 3B06  |  Source PDF p. 24","source":{"name":"3B06 Examples.docx","sha256":"d583ea1038f9b46820eba3be68c1c0973a5d80238ac0c6e625df0427ca7931ae","block":55},"id":"ex-3B06-4","chapter":"3B06"},"ex-3B06-5":{"number":"5","title":"Radar","question":"<p>A radar system consists of a transmitter and a receiver. The transmitter sends out short pulses of microwaves. If the pulses hit an object such as an aircraft, some of the pulses bounce back to the receiver.</p><p>A radar pulse bounces back from a plane 4 × 10<sup>–5</sup> s after it is transmitted. What is the distance of the plane from the radar station?</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/870287a45dd106c463d8247c.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:249.2px\"/></p>","answer":"<p>Time for pulse to travel from transmitter to plane = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>4</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>×</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>–</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = 2 × 10<sup>–5</sup> s</p><p>Distance = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = (3 × 10<sup>8</sup>)(2 × 10<sup>–5</sup>) = 6000 m</p>","tips":"","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 3B  |  Chapter 3B06  |  Source PDF p. 34","source":{"name":"3B06 Examples.docx","sha256":"d583ea1038f9b46820eba3be68c1c0973a5d80238ac0c6e625df0427ca7931ae","block":66},"id":"ex-3B06-5","chapter":"3B06"},"ex-3B06-6":{"number":"6","title":"Using walkie-talkies","question":"<p>In Hong Kong, there are two types of walkie-talkies for public use, one using 27-MHz radio waves and the other 409-MHz radio waves.</p><p>(a) Find the wavelengths of the two radio waves in air.</p><p>(b) Peter and Tony go hiking with walkie-talkies. As Peter reaches <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>B</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> as shown, he receives signals from Tony at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> which is out of his sight.</p><p>(i) State the wave phenomenon which allows Peter to receive radio</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/f2840ef8a3d9831a4c968982.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:213.4px\"/></p><p>signals from Tony.</p><p>(ii) Which type of walkie–talkie has a better performance in this case? Explain briefly.</p><p>(iii) Explain why the reception is affected when a large cargo ship passes near Peter.</p>","answer":"<p>(a) Apply <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>f</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>λ .</p><p>Wavelength of 27-MHz radio waves = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>f</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>×</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>8</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>7</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>×</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>6</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = 11.1 m</p><p>Wavelength of 409-MHz radio waves = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>f</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>×</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>8</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>4</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>9</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>×</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><msup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>6</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = 0.733 m</p><p>(b) (i) Diffraction</p><p>(ii) The one using 27 MHz radio waves has a better performance since the radio waves have a longer wavelength and are diffracted more around the headland.</p><p>(iii) The radio waves reflected by the cargo ship interfere with the waves coming directly from Tony, so the reception is affected.</p>","tips":"","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 3B  |  Chapter 3B06  |  Source PDF p. 35","source":{"name":"3B06 Examples.docx","sha256":"d583ea1038f9b46820eba3be68c1c0973a5d80238ac0c6e625df0427ca7931ae","block":74},"id":"ex-3B06-6","chapter":"3B06"}}